Abhilfe:
========
* _memset:    Einfaches C-Coding
* _memcpy:    Einfaches C-Coding
x _mkdir:     Aufruf ueber system() wie im XENIX-Coding in aix.c
x _rmdir:     Aufruf ueber system() wie im XENIX-Coding in aix.c
x _strerror:  const char *const sys_errlist[];
              int sys_nerr;

              The global error list sys_errlist[], which can be indexed
              by errno, can be used to obtain the error message without
              the newline. The largest message number provided in the
              table is sys_nerr-1. Be care- ful when directly accessing
              this list, because new error values may not have been
              added to sys_errlist[]. The use of sys_errlist[] is
              nowadays deprecated; use strerror(3) instead.

* _vfprintf:

              Difficult, because we need vfprintf to return the number
              of chars written ... With fseek/ftell it could be
              possible to implement it.

              start = ftell(iop);
              /**fprintf(iop, fmt, ?);**/
            _doprnt(fmt, ap, iop);
            end   = ftell(iop);
            rc  = end - start;

fprintf(iop, fmt, args)
FILE *iop;
char *fmt;
{
        _doprnt(fmt, &args, iop);
        return(ferror(iop)? EOF: 0);
}

vfprintf(iop, fmt, ap)  _doprnt(fmt, ap, iop)

<https://www.unix.com/man_page/bsd/3s/vfprintf/> shows that these
functions are available in 1988 BSD Unixes: In 4.2 BSD. It is from 1983.

<https://gunkies.org/wiki/4.2_BSD#How_do_I_get_this_to_run.3F.21>

But it might be, that the return code is *not* the number of characters written
===> Switch to BSD 4.2 and include the ftell() logic;


* _strchr:  Einfaches C-Coding

x _S_ISDIR:   #define S_ISDIR(x)  ((x) & S_IFMT) == S_IFDIR )
x _S_ISREG:   #define S_ISREG(x)  ((x) & S_IFMT) == S_IFREG )
x _rename:    Wie in aix.c fuer XENIX und Konsorten implementiert


